{:now [{:claim "A manifold with a line element"
        :modern "A Riemannian manifold is a smooth manifold with a positive definite symmetric 2-tensor g; lengths, angles, geodesics and curvature all come from g."
        :tex "ds^2 = g_{ij}\\,dx^i dx^j"}
       {:claim "Curvature in every surface direction"
        :modern "Riemann's curvature at a point in a surface direction is today's sectional curvature K(σ) = R(X,Y,Y,X)/|X∧Y|², read off the Riemann curvature tensor; in two dimensions it is Gauss' K."
        :tex "K(X,Y) = \\frac{\\langle R(X,Y)Y, X\\rangle}{|X|^2|Y|^2 - \\langle X,Y\\rangle^2}"}
       {:claim "Geodesics spread according to curvature"
        :modern "The Jacobi equation J'' + K J = 0 measures how nearby geodesics converge (K > 0) or diverge (K < 0)."
        :tex "\\ddot J + K\\,J = 0"}]
 :fields [{:field :mechanics-physics :label "Physics" :how "General relativity (1915) makes gravity the curvature of a Lorentzian metric on spacetime, determined by matter through Einstein's equations: Riemann's 'binding forces'." :example "The perihelion of Mercury and the bending of starlight, 1919."}
          {:field :differential-geometry :how "Riemannian geometry: comparison theorems, Gauss-Bonnet, the classification of surfaces by curvature, Ricci flow and the Poincaré conjecture (2003)." :example "A complete surface with K ≥ c > 0 is compact (Bonnet-Myers)."}
          {:field :logic-computing :label "Computing" :how "Geodesics on meshes and statistical manifolds: shortest paths on surfaces, optimisation along the Fisher metric (natural gradient)." :example "Geodesic distance on a 3D scan for shape matching."}
          {:field :topology :label "Topology" :how "Curvature constrains topology: the integral of K over a closed surface is 2π times its Euler characteristic." :example "Every metric on the torus has total curvature 0."}]
 :tangents [{:series :physics :hook "From ds² to Einstein's field equations: is physical space curved? Riemann left it to experiment, Einstein answered with gravity." :link "planned"}
            {:series :erlangen :hook "Klein's Erlangen programme (1872) classifies geometries by groups; Riemann's metric geometries mostly have no symmetry at all, the other road." :link "/erlangen-program/"}
            {:series :projective :hook "Cayley and Klein recover Riemann's constant-curvature spaces inside projective geometry, with a conic as the absolute." :link "/projective/"}]
 :texts [{:author "Bernhard Riemann" :work "Ueber die Hypothesen, welche der Geometrie zu Grunde liegen (Abh. Ges. Wiss. Göttingen 13, 1868)" :year 1868 :url "https://www.emis.de/classics/Riemann/Geom.pdf" :kind :archive :lang "de"}
         {:author "Bernhard Riemann" :work "On the Hypotheses which lie at the Bases of Geometry, tr. W. K. Clifford (Nature 8, 1873)" :year 1873 :url "https://www.emis.de/classics/Riemann/WKCGeom.pdf" :kind :translation :lang "en"}
